#type	charge(e)	radius(A)	epsilon(kJ.mol-1)		mass(g.mol-1)	transferIMP(kJ.mol-1.A-2)	Hydrophobicity
#
# SHG, THG1 and YHH -- the hydroxyl hydrogens of serine, threonine and tyrosine
# -- carry the Lennard-Jones parameters of AMBER's amide hydrogen (R* 0.6 A,
# epsilon 0.065689 kJ/mol) instead of AMBER's own 0/0.
#
# AMBER really does give type HO no Lennard-Jones at all, and can afford to:
# its exclusion list removes every 1-2 and 1-3 pair and scales 1-4 down, so a
# hydroxyl hydrogen never meets an acceptor without a term that holds it off.
# BioSpring excludes the pairs its SPRING network joins (Particle.cpp, guarded
# by spring.enable) and nothing else, so two residues' atoms are never
# excluded from each other. A hydroxyl hydrogen at +0.41 e then faces a
# carboxylate oxygen at -0.80 e with no repulsion whatsoever, and the Coulomb
# term diverges as 1/r while the springs holding the rest of the residue only
# grow as r. Measured on example 072: 21 such hydrogens, 136 attractive pairs
# that no spring joins, the closest THR82:HG1 -- GLU69:OE1 at 1.653 A pulling
# with 171 kJ.mol-1.A-1. The run reached 3.2e21 kJ/mol at every timestep from
# 4 fs down to 0.5 fs; with this floor it settles at 283 kJ/mol.
#
# The honest alternative is to exclude 1-4 pairs properly, which needs a
# chemical bond graph BioSpring does not keep. Until then this floor is what
# makes --rigidbody usable with coulomb.enable.
#
#
# COLUMN 7, HYDROPHOBICITY -- filled, and what it means here
#
# The pairwise hydrophobic term reads this column. It was 0.0 on all 337 types,
# as it is in every force field shipped with BioSpring, so the term had no
# effect on any all-atom model: hydrophobicity.enable = 1 changed nothing.
#
# The law is E_ij = -h_i h_j L exp(-r_ij/L), Israelachvili & Pashley
# (Nature 300:341, 1982), with L = hydrophobicity.decaylength, default 10 A.
#
# WHAT WAS DISTRIBUTED. Fauchere & Pliska's octanol/water partition scale for
# amino acid SIDE CHAINS (Eur. J. Med. Chem. 18:369-375, 1983), relative to
# glycine -- the same experimental numbers example 058 uses at one bead per
# residue, so the two resolutions agree by construction:
#
#     eps_R = 5.708 * max(pi_R, 0)  kJ.mol-1    (5.708 = 2.303 RT at 298 K)
#     h_R   = sqrt(eps_R / 10 A)
#
# HOW, and why it is a SUM and not a share of squares. L = 10 A is much larger
# than a residue, so exp(-r_ij/L) is nearly the same for every atom pair of two
# residues in contact, and E_RR' ~= -(sum_i h_i)(sum_j h_j) L exp(-r/L). The
# all-atom values therefore have to SUM to the residue's for the two models to
# agree at long range. They do, to 1e-16.
#
# WHICH ATOMS. Side-chain carbon and sulfur, one equal share each; nitrogen,
# oxygen, every hydrogen and the whole backbone carry zero. C and S against
# N and O is Eisenberg & McLachlan's classification (Nature 319:199, 1986).
# The backbone carries nothing because the scale is a side-chain scale measured
# relative to glycine, so the backbone is already subtracted out of it. Equal
# shares rather than shares of surface because there is no surface anywhere in
# this law, and putting one in would invent a quantity it does not use.
#
# The partial charge in column 2 was tried as the criterion -- Kapcha & Rossky
# (J. Mol. Biol. 426:484, 2014) build an atomic scale out of exactly that -- and
# it does not work with these charges. Leucine's CG comes out at +0.3531 and
# both its CD at -0.4121, so the archetypal hydrophobic side chain is classified
# polar, and grouping each carbon with its hydrogens does not save it
# (CG + HG = +0.317). That is amber99sb's charge fitting on branched aliphatics.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, measured on a 34-residue beta-alpha-beta motif (552 beads),
# unforced over 60 ps, against the same run with the term off:
#
#   term off              6/6 sheet, 19/21 helix, 1.99 A RMSD
#   on, L = 10 A          6/6,       18/21,       4.11 A     <- over-compacts
#   on, L = 4 A           6/6,       19/21,       1.87-2.70 A
#
# At the published 10 A the term pulls a native structure 2.1 A off where it
# should sit. At 4 A it leaves it alone -- and then it drives nothing: pulled
# open and released, recovery is identical with the term and without it. At 10 A
# it does drive, and exactly as a hydrophobic term should: after a pull the
# heavy-atom RMSD comes back to 6.86 A instead of 8.08 A, with the SAME helix
# count. Compaction is its job; secondary structure is the hydrogen bond's.
#
# So decaylength is the knob, the trade is real, and neither end of it is free.
# It does not fight the dynamic hydrogen bond: this term acts on C and S, that
# one on N-H...O, and the two atom sets are disjoint. Measured together, the
# sheet stays 6/6 and the helix 18-19/21 whatever the decay length.
#
# Note the GPU computes this term but does not REPORT its energy: no pairwise
# energy is summed on the device (steric and electrostatic are missing too).
# The forces are on both backends; the number is only on the CPU.
#
